WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:04.400
Film Courage: What’s the difference between
having a big ego and a strong ego as a director?

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:08.050
Kenneth Castillo, Filmmaker: That’s a great
question.

00:00:08.050 --> 00:00:12.419
Well having a big ego comes from insecurity
right?

00:00:12.419 --> 00:00:16.270
Having a strong ego is being confident in
what you are doing.

00:00:16.270 --> 00:00:19.070
You don’t have to beat people over the head
with it.

00:00:19.070 --> 00:00:22.660
One thing I said with my latest film is I’m
not trying to beat people over the head with

00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:23.660
my film.

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:26.610
I’m trying to break their heart with it.

00:00:26.610 --> 00:00:29.470
And it really comes down to approach.

00:00:29.470 --> 00:00:35.660
But it really is essentially about a fine
line between arrogance and confidence right?

00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:37.620
And people know the difference.

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:43.790
But the problem is directors are fed that
ego on set whether they know what they are

00:00:43.790 --> 00:00:46.340
doing or they don’t, they are fed that.

00:00:46.340 --> 00:00:49.690
Because people just assume that they know
what they’re doing.

00:00:49.690 --> 00:00:52.690
For me I don’t like to manipulate actors.

00:00:52.690 --> 00:00:54.960
I don’t feel I need to manipulate actors.

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:59.980
I don’t need to cause havoc between two
people to get a performance because the audience

00:00:59.980 --> 00:01:03.489
knows only what I show them right here.

00:01:03.489 --> 00:01:06.370
They don’t know all of this, they don’t
need to know all of that.

00:01:06.370 --> 00:01:11.290
So to me confidence is not even the right
word.

00:01:11.290 --> 00:01:17.030
If there is a better word for confidence I
would just say being able to communicate your

00:01:17.030 --> 00:01:21.659
vision in a very clear and concise way.

00:01:21.659 --> 00:01:31.250
If you know how to do that you don’t need
to be that flamboyant.

00:01:31.250 --> 00:01:33.380
But a director is one thing.

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:37.700
An actor to a director, they are never going
to call a director on that you know what I’m

00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:38.700
saying?

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:39.849
Whether he is arrogant or confident, a director
is never going to be called out on that, whether

00:01:39.849 --> 00:01:41.549
he is arrogant or confident, a director is
never going to be called out on that on a

00:01:41.549 --> 00:01:43.409
professional set.

00:01:43.409 --> 00:01:46.980
Everyone assumes that they are there because
they are supposed to be there so you have

00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:49.030
this kind of advantage.

00:01:49.030 --> 00:01:52.170
But where it really starts to boil down is
how you communicate with the actors and when

00:01:52.170 --> 00:01:58.200
my crew starts to engage, then I know we have
something.

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:02.009
I always come by the crew because they see
a million things right?

00:02:02.009 --> 00:02:04.450
They see a million movies, they are on sets
a million times.

00:02:04.450 --> 00:02:08.179
So if I see them engaging and watching the
actors performance, then I’m like “Okay,

00:02:08.179 --> 00:02:09.269
we got something.

00:02:09.269 --> 00:02:12.180
The crew is interested, we have something
here.”

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:15.580
Or they are trying to make it better, they
are trying to take it and make it a little

00:02:15.580 --> 00:02:19.000
bit better.

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:20.569
But that is essentially it.

00:02:20.569 --> 00:02:25.510
I always tell every filmmaker, I go there
are three tenants I live by and I formed them

00:02:25.510 --> 00:02:31.910
by watching other directors that didn’t
go on to do anything.

00:02:31.910 --> 00:02:34.490
And one is “Don’t let your ego be bigger
than your career.”

00:02:34.490 --> 00:02:40.010
Two - “Don’t make promises you can’t
keep, especially to cast and crew.”

00:02:40.010 --> 00:02:43.690
And the last one is “When it’s all said
and done better let there be more be said

00:02:43.690 --> 00:02:45.550
than done.”

00:02:45.550 --> 00:02:50.180
Lead by example.

00:02:50.180 --> 00:02:55.069
Especially in the social media age a lot of
people can post anything about everyone.

00:02:55.069 --> 00:02:58.019
Everyone is a producer, everybody is this,
that or the other.

00:02:58.019 --> 00:03:00.700
But when you look beyond it, is the work there?

00:03:00.700 --> 00:03:03.870
That’s what I always tell people, don’t
look at the surface, look deeper.

00:03:03.870 --> 00:03:06.180
Don’t be so fooled so easily.

00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:10.180
You can tell…someone asked me recently “This
guy is a producer.”

00:03:10.180 --> 00:03:12.800
If he’s a producer, then what has he produced?

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:15.319
He’s like “Well I don’t want to ask
him that.

00:03:15.319 --> 00:03:16.350
It would be insulting him.”

00:03:16.350 --> 00:03:17.660
And I’m like “Why are you insulting him?

00:03:17.660 --> 00:03:21.570
You’re only insulting him if he doesn’t
have anything.”

00:03:21.570 --> 00:03:22.570
I go “Then you know.

00:03:22.570 --> 00:03:24.700
I know it feels good to have someone go I
want to help you, I’m a producer.

00:03:24.700 --> 00:03:27.330
I know that feels good but don’t be fooled
by that.

00:03:27.330 --> 00:03:29.870
But just ask them “What have you done?”

00:03:29.870 --> 00:03:31.290
We get asked that question all of the time.

00:03:31.290 --> 00:03:33.880
When I tell someone I’m a filmmaker “What
have you done?”

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:35.420
“Well I’ve done this, this and this.”

00:03:35.420 --> 00:03:36.420
“Where can I watch it?”

00:03:36.420 --> 00:03:38.409
“You can watch it here, here and here.”

00:03:38.409 --> 00:03:39.790
“Oh, okay.”

00:03:39.790 --> 00:03:42.190
But if someone says they’re a producer and
you go “What have you produced?”

00:03:42.190 --> 00:03:45.909
And they get upset and they get their ego
damaged that’s because they haven’t done

00:03:45.909 --> 00:03:47.930
anything.

00:03:47.930 --> 00:03:49.459
So don’t be fooled by that.

00:03:49.459 --> 00:03:55.610
Film Courage: What if we took it a step back
and we see the difference between ego and

00:03:55.610 --> 00:04:00.090
sort of a strong sense in even wanting to
be a filmmaker?

00:04:00.090 --> 00:04:04.250
Kenneth: Well…look…you have to have…there
is a certain amount of narcissism you have

00:04:04.250 --> 00:04:05.760
to have.

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:12.069
To me it’s about controlling that and there
is a certain amount of maybe even arrogance

00:04:12.069 --> 00:04:15.349
to say “I’m going to make something that
people are going to care about.”

00:04:15.349 --> 00:04:17.949
So you have to have just a tiny bit of that.

00:04:17.949 --> 00:04:24.659
But more so than that what I say is it’s
more of a naivety and I think that having

00:04:24.659 --> 00:04:29.360
the naivety during your first film is a strength,
it’s not a weakness because you don’t

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:34.069
know what you’re up against and that’s
a good thing because if you knew that maybe

00:04:34.069 --> 00:04:37.360
you would never get started and maybe that
would save you a bunch of time, heartbreak

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:42.580
and money but if this is what you want to
do and you don’t know what is ahead of you,

00:04:42.580 --> 00:04:47.080
you don’t know what is involved, that will
drive you to the end of your film (at least

00:04:47.080 --> 00:04:50.460
your first film) and then when you get to
the end of it you may be like “Wow, I’m

00:04:50.460 --> 00:04:52.909
not going to do that again.”

00:04:52.909 --> 00:04:57.379
Or you are going to be like “I can’t wait
to do that again!”

00:04:57.379 --> 00:05:01.580
Regardless of where it goes, if it went to
Sundance or it got distribution, regardless.

00:05:01.580 --> 00:05:05.749
If your attitude after that is “Man that
was hard as heck but that was awesome.

00:05:05.749 --> 00:05:07.949
I have to do that again.”

00:05:07.949 --> 00:05:13.039
Then A - there is something wrong with you
and B - your a filmmaker, then go, go, go.

00:05:13.039 --> 00:05:16.340
Film Courage: And it’s okay to have there
be something wrong with you because there

00:05:16.340 --> 00:05:17.340
are degrees.

00:05:17.340 --> 00:05:21.509
They say that all artists are crazy or selfish
(whatever).

00:05:21.509 --> 00:05:22.509
But there are degrees of it.

00:05:22.509 --> 00:05:23.509
It’s not all negative.

00:05:23.509 --> 00:05:27.430
It means they want to create something.

00:05:27.430 --> 00:05:32.430
Kenneth: I have a balance because like I said
I have a family and I’m one of the few.

00:05:32.430 --> 00:05:36.529
I’m one of the few people I know that has
a family and has done both.

00:05:36.529 --> 00:05:40.699
That’s the other question I get asked is
“Well how do you do it?”

00:05:40.699 --> 00:05:42.539
And well I ask them “Do you have a family?”

00:05:42.539 --> 00:05:43.539
“No.”

00:05:43.539 --> 00:05:45.240
“Do you have a job?”

00:05:45.240 --> 00:05:46.240
“No.”

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:47.240
I’m like “Okay?

00:05:47.240 --> 00:05:48.619
You have no responsibility and you’re asking
me how to do it?”

00:05:48.619 --> 00:05:51.000
I go “You go do it.”

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:52.599
I go “Do you have one of these?”

00:05:52.599 --> 00:05:54.169
And I grab their phone.

00:05:54.169 --> 00:05:55.169
“Go do it.

00:05:55.169 --> 00:05:57.229
If you want me to tell you how to make a million
dollars?

00:05:57.229 --> 00:05:58.699
Do you want me to tell you how to get into
Sundance?

00:05:58.699 --> 00:06:00.810
Do you want me to tell you how to get into
a theater?

00:06:00.810 --> 00:06:05.240
I don’t know how to do those things.

00:06:05.240 --> 00:06:11.509
But the fact that you are sitting there asking
me when you have time, energy and a cell phone,

00:06:11.509 --> 00:06:13.360
make something.

00:06:13.360 --> 00:06:14.449
Is it going to be great?

00:06:14.449 --> 00:06:16.590
Probably not your first time out.

00:06:16.590 --> 00:06:17.729
But that’s part of it man.

00:06:17.729 --> 00:06:20.569
You’ve go to do the crap to do some cool
stuff.”

00:06:20.569 --> 00:06:22.550
I’ve done seven films.

00:06:22.550 --> 00:06:25.270
Not all of them are great.

00:06:25.270 --> 00:06:27.389
The ones that aren’t great, that was my
fault.

00:06:27.389 --> 00:06:31.939
No one is to blame accept me on those ones
you know.

00:06:31.939 --> 00:06:35.740
But everyone of them is a learning experience
and everyone of them feeds the next one.

